    <edge source="AA" target="conditions hivernales severes aux moyennes latitudes" id="verified_rel_005">
      <data key="d10">verified_rel_005</data>
      <data key="d11">INFLUENCES</data>
      <data key="d12">0.95</data>
      <data key="d13">verified_evaluation_annotation</data>
      <data key="d14">MAY_INCREASE</data>
      <data key="d15">53</data>
      <data key="d16">38</data>
      <data key="d17">paragraph_based_00235</data>
      <data key="d18">Arctic amplification (AA) is a mechanism that may lead to an increase in severe winter weather in the midlatitudes (Cohen et al., 2014; Francis et al., 2018; Francis and Vavrus, 2012; Vavrus, 2018), but its potential effect is intertwined with other hemispheric drivers of decadal variability, and the quantification of its influence remains debated (Blackport and Screen, 2020; Cohen et al., 2020; Francis, 2017).</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AA" target="Europe" id="cooccur_0079">
      <data key="d10">cooccur_0079</data>
      <data key="d11">CO_OCCURS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d12">0.560904</data>
      <data key="d13">gazetteer_cooccurrence</data>
      <data key="d19">3</data>
      <data key="d15">26</data>
      <data key="d16">11</data>
      <data key="d17">paragraph_based_00089</data>
      <data key="d18">In Europe, Francis and Vavrus (2012) first proposed that AA and the subsequent sea-ice loss would lead to a slower eastward propagation of Rossby waves, due to weakened zonal winds and increased wave amplitude. This mechanism could potentially cause an increase in extreme events, including cold spells, due to prolonged</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AA" target="AMOC" id="cooccur_0108">
      <data key="d10">cooccur_0108</data>
      <data key="d11">CO_OCCURS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d12">0.537889</data>
      <data key="d13">gazetteer_cooccurrence</data>
      <data key="d19">2</data>
      <data key="d15">89</data>
      <data key="d16">74</data>
      <data key="d17">paragraph_based_00470</data>
      <data key="d18">Nevertheless, there are uncertainties surrounding the impact of Arctic amplification and the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC) on cold extremes. For instance, these phenomena could increase the meanders of the jet stream, leading to more frequent and intense cold spells (Geen et al., 2023), although th</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AA" target="France" id="cooccur_0109">
      <data key="d10">cooccur_0109</data>
      <data key="d11">CO_OCCURS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d12">0.537889</data>
      <data key="d13">gazetteer_cooccurrence</data>
      <data key="d19">2</data>
      <data key="d15">53</data>
      <data key="d16">38</data>
      <data key="d17">paragraph_based_00235</data>
      <data key="d18">However, uncertainties remain about the potential dynamical effects of climate change on severe winter midlatitude weather (Cohen et al., 2020; Horton et al., 2015; Overland et al., 2016; Shepherd, 2015). Arctic amplification (AA) is a mechanism that may lead to an increase in severe winter weather in the midlatitudes </data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AA" target="atmospheric circulation" id="cooccur_0110">
      <data key="d10">cooccur_0110</data>
      <data key="d11">CO_OCCURS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d12">0.537889</data>
      <data key="d13">gazetteer_cooccurrence</data>
      <data key="d19">2</data>
      <data key="d15">74</data>
      <data key="d16">59</data>
      <data key="d17">paragraph_based_00391</data>
      <data key="d18">The direct thermodynamical warming is expected to be further amplified by feedbacks such as snow-albedo effects and because of the weakening of cold air advection in the mid-latitudes due to Arctic amplification, which acts to also reduce mid-latitude wintertime temperature variance (Screen, 2014; Schneider et al., 201</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AA" target="jet stream" id="cooccur_0111">
      <data key="d10">cooccur_0111</data>
      <data key="d11">CO_OCCURS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d12">0.537889</data>
      <data key="d13">gazetteer_cooccurrence</data>
      <data key="d19">2</data>
      <data key="d15">26</data>
      <data key="d16">11</data>
      <data key="d17">paragraph_based_00090</data>
      <data key="d18">In response, Wallace et al. (2014) argued that, despite the possibility of such linkages, cold air outbreaks in the mid-latitudes and the recently observed decrease in the rate of cold records relative to hot ones are not incompatible with global warming. Therefore, these events should not be the central focus of the c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AMOC" target="Europe" id="cooccur_0112">
      <data key="d10">cooccur_0112</data>
      <data key="d11">CO_OCCURS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d12">0.537889</data>
      <data key="d13">gazetteer_cooccurrence</data>
      <data key="d19">2</data>
      <data key="d15">25</data>
      <data key="d16">10</data>
      <data key="d17">paragraph_based_00081</data>
      <data key="d18">However, the study also warns about the capacity of models to accurately reproduce the frequency of CAOs in the North Atlantic, supposedly because of the modelling of complex processes such as the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC). In Western Europe, De Vries et al. (2012) state that the intensity of c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AR6" target="IPCC" id="cooccur_0113">
      <data key="d10">cooccur_0113</data>
      <data key="d11">CO_OCCURS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d12">0.537889</data>
      <data key="d13">gazetteer_cooccurrence</data>
      <data key="d19">2</data>
      <data key="d15">24</data>
      <data key="d16">9</data>
      <data key="d17">paragraph_based_00076</data>
      <data key="d18">Extreme cold events are projected to further decrease with global warming (Seneviratne et al. 2021; Committee on Extreme Weather Events and Climate Change Attribution et al. 2016). With increasing warming levels, as stated in the IPCC AR6: "the frequency and intensity of [. . . ] cold extremes will continue to decrease</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Atlantic ridge" target="NAO" id="cooccur_0114">
      <data key="d10">cooccur_0114</data>
      <data key="d11">CO_OCCURS_WITH</data>
      <data key="d12">0.537889</data>
      <data key="d13">gazetteer_cooccurrence</data>
      <data key="d19">2</data>
      <data key="d15">21</data>
      <data key="d16">6</data>
      <data key="d17">paragraph_based_00062</data>
      <data key="d18">The jet stream is tightly linked to weather regimes, which are major drivers of midlatitude weather. Weather regime patterns are defined as recurrent and persistent circulation anomalies, in other words, quasi-stationary states of the atmosphere (Hannachi et al. 2017). They are obtained through statistical analysis (us</data>
    </edge>
